course: dictionary
lesson: 7628
title: One word, many uses: process
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A process is a series of steps or changes that happen one after another to reach a result. As a noun, it can be a natural process, like «a process called fusion» inside the Sun, or a method of steps, like long division.
explain: Look at the words around process to find its meaning. «Plants make their own food in a process called photosynthesis.» The word called tells you a name is coming, and making food tells you it is a set of changes that lead to a result.
explain: Process can also be a verb. To process something means to deal with it or change it in steps, like computers process data. The phrase «in the process of» means while you are doing something, so you are in the process of learning when you are still learning.
fact: A process is a series of steps or changes that happen one after another.
fact: «Photosynthesis» is a process in which plants use light to make their own food.
fact: «Longshore drift» is a process: waves hitting a beach at an angle move sand along the coast.
fact: To process something means to deal with it in steps, like processing food or information.
fact: «In the process of» means while doing something, as in «in the process of learning».
exercise: «Plants make their own food in a process called photosynthesis.» What is a process? | A kind of plant | *A series of steps or changes that lead to a result | A pot used for cooking | A loud noise
exercise: «Waves hitting a beach at an angle move sand along the coast, a process called longshore drift.» What happens to the sand? | Turns into glass straight away | Stays still forever | Floats up into the air | *Is moved along the coast by the waves
exercise: Sam says, «I am in the process of learning to ride a bike.» What does in the process of mean here? | Finished and done with bikes for good | *While doing something, still learning | Riding a bike very fast | Never going to try again
test: What does it mean to process information? | To ignore it completely | To forget it quickly | *To deal with it in steps | To shout it out loud
test: In «processed food», what does processed mean? | Grown in a garden | Sold with no change at all | *Changed in steps, often in a factory | Cooked over a campfire
exam: Which description of a process is correct? | A single loud sound that stops quickly | A type of cloud that brings rain | *Steps that happen one after another to reach a result | A person who is very old
